WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.680
Film Courage: What three things does a great
story have to have?

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:07.509
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author, Publisher, Producer:
What three things?

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:16.170
Well it has to have a hook that gets people
instantly involved in the story and that’s

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.470
a huge part of the story itself.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:25.679
And it’s got to have a very strong character
in the story that you car about and other

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:32.570
than that it has to have twists and turns
that lead to a surprise ending and if I had

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:37.579
to just say three things, I guess that’s
what I would say the three things are.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:43.870
Every story needs that because a story about
nothing is not going to hold anyone’s interest.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.430
And sometimes writers when they begin their
careers think that if they just write, they

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.870
can write about anything but the truth is
they need to write from their heart about

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.610
things that matter to everyone and if they
do that you can hardly go wrong.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.160
Because stories are really not about words
or word choice or anything like that.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:11.910
They’re about conveying the power of a character
facing a dilemma that you have no idea how

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:19.000
he or she will resolve and when you do that
you’ve got everyone’s attention.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.290
And in ancient times there was a thing called
The Oral Tradition which I used to teach as

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:28.310
a professor of Homeric Greek.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:36.550
The Iliad and the Odyssey were sung at campfires
and everyone in the culture knew the stories.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:44.020
We are publishing a book right now on Homeric
song and how it worked and how it held culture

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.020
together.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.780
And my first book those…I call those stories
the shield of memory and it was because of

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:57.040
those stories that a person knew how to deal
with himself in battle or when facing an attacking

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:05.120
boar or when facing an angry wife you know
or when facing pillagers trying to burn down

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.640
his village.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.310
He would instantly think of the story of Heracles
who did this or that or the story of Aegean

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.140
who did this and that and that’s all they
had.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.270
They didn’t have books for learning.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:24.950
It was all passed along through the oral tradition
and I think stories have never failed to play

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:29.510
that role in human life and when you think
about it you know “What’s your story?”

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:37.420
is probably the most human response to any
encounter and it goes from the court of law

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.590
where the jury is trying to decide which of
the two stories do they believe to a political

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:50.710
campaign where the voters are making that
decision to a first date where you are going

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:52.630
are going “Do I believe his story?

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.170
I just don’t believe it?

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.710
I can’t buy his story?”

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:01.140
That’s the ultimate human turn down, you
can’t buy the story.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.690
And it goes through everything.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:10.350
Advertising is conveying stories that people
will want to buy the product.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:16.610
This is how humans operate on a daily basis
so to me it’s absolutely amazing that an

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.750
industry has been created where people will
pay millions of dollars for stories and where

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.520
stories can basically conquer the world and
I believe unite the world.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.250
I mean look at all the work we are now doing
with China in the movie business.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:38.370
I just saw Lara Croft in Tomb Raider (the
new version of it) where the male lead is

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:46.140
Chinese and she is Western and clearly as
a producer I’m watching it going “This

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.069
was a Chinese financed movie,” because I
understand how it works for the market.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:57.060
The Chinese hero makes it perfect for the
Chinese market and he looks pretty good but

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:03.040
she’s the real protagonist in the story
and she’s great for the Western market and

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.420
she’s a woman so it’s all very contemporary,
etc.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:13.420
So you see the structure behind it is actually
a cultural change because now the values of

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:19.570
the West are being inserted in the Chinese
market with the Chinese co-opted cooperating

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:26.770
with them and as I believe China’s becoming
more capitalistic all the time partly through

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:33.750
the influence of movies the audience wants
to see individual people doing what they want

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:39.919
to do in life and going out there and kicking
a$$ and not being under the thumb of some

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.120
Emperor or despot.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.730
So I think this is why the whole storytelling
thing is so exciting because it really is

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.520
a universal experience.

